  'weekend',
  'The TSX Rebounded as Canada Lost Jobs, and the Labour Market Split Beneath the Surface',
  'Friday’s market recovery masked a third consecutive monthly employment decline, a shift from private to public hiring, and sharply different risks across Canadian households.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>Canada lost 4,700 jobs in October,</strong><span> while unemployment rose to 6.7% and the employment rate fell to 60.5%.</span></li>
<li><strong>Private payrolls declined by 53,000,</strong><span> as public employment increased by 50,000, masking a change in job composition.</span></li>
<li><strong>The youth unemployment rate reached 13.7%,</strong><span> while Ontario and Alberta each recorded 7.3% unemployment.</span></li>
<li><strong>The TSX ended Friday at 35,789.09,</strong><span> recovering 0.5% as miners and technology shares advanced.</span></li>
<li><strong>The advisory question has shifted from inflation alone</strong><span> to household income resilience, liquidity and the timing of further rate increases.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>Friday''s employment report changed the meaning of the week''s Canadian market rebound. The S&amp;P/TSX Composite closed at 35,789.09, gaining 183.82 points on Friday and about half a per cent over the week. Yet the underlying labour figures gave investors a less reassuring picture of domestic demand. Canada lost 4,700 jobs in October, the third consecutive monthly decline, and unemployment rose to 6.7%. The market''s relief and the economy''s weakness are not contradictory: a softer jobs market can reduce expectations for another Bank of Canada rate increase even as it threatens household earnings.</p>
<h2>The Headline Conceals a Change in Who Is Hiring</h2>
<p>Statistics Canada reported a decline of 53,000 private sector employees alongside a gain of 50,000 public sector employees. Those movements nearly cancelled one another in the aggregate, but they carry different implications for business investment and tax receipts. Total employment was virtually unchanged in October, following losses of 68,000 in September and 42,000 in August. The employment rate slipped to 60.5%, its third straight monthly decline. Over twelve months, employment rose just 17,000, or 0.1%.</p>
<p>Weakness was not universal. Wholesale and retail trade lost 29,000 positions in October, while construction added 16,000 and educational services gained 15,000. Average hourly wages rose 2.2% year over year to $37.89, slower than September''s 2.3% increase. None of these individual readings proves a recession, but together they suggest a labour market that is no longer generating a broad cushion against higher borrowing costs.</p>
<h2>One National Rate, Fifteen Different Views</h2>
<p>Provincial differences matter more than a single Canada-wide unemployment figure suggests. Manitoba''s rate was 5.0%, Quebec''s 5.7%, Ontario''s 7.3% and Alberta''s 7.3%. Newfoundland and Labrador stood at 8.7%. Alberta''s rate increased by 0.9 percentage points in October, while Quebec''s fell by 0.3 points. A nationally diversified investment portfolio does not mean a household''s employment exposure is diversified: salary, home value and small-business revenue may all depend on the same regional economy.</p>
<p>The age split is sharper still. Youth unemployment reached 13.7%, against 5.8% for core-aged men, 5.3% for core-aged women and 5.5% for workers aged 55 and older. These categories have different population bases, so the chart below presents them as comparisons rather than components of a national total.</p><div class="hdq-chart"><div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #d0d0d0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;"><div style="background:#f5f5f5;border-bottom:1px solid #d0d0d0;padding:10px 14px;display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap;"><span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#111;">CANADA''S UNEVEN LABOUR MARKET</span><span style="font-size:20px;font-weight:700;">6.7%</span><span style="font-size:11px;color:#777;margin-left:auto;">OCTOBER 2026 | UNEMPLOYMENT RATE</span></div><div style="padding:12px 14px 8px;"><script>
(function(){
 var script=document.currentScript,ns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg";
 var svg=document.createElementNS(ns,"svg");
 svg.setAttribute("viewBox","0 0 680 300");svg.setAttribute("width","100%");
 svg.setAttribute("role","img");svg.setAttribute("aria-label","Fifteen unemployment rate observations: Canada, ten provinces and four age-sex groups, October 2026");
 function el(tag,attrs,txt){var e=document.createElementNS(ns,tag);Object.keys(attrs).forEach(function(k){e.setAttribute(k,attrs[k])});if(txt!==undefined)e.textContent=txt;svg.appendChild(e);return e}
 var data=[["Canada",6.7],["N.L.",8.7],["P.E.I.",7.4],["N.S.",6.2],["N.B.",7.4],["Quebec",5.7],["Ontario",7.3],["Manitoba",5.0],["Sask.",5.9],["Alberta",7.3],["B.C.",6.3],["Youth 15–24",13.7],["Men 25–54",5.8],["Women 25–54",5.3],["Age 55+",5.5]];
 var x0=118,plotW=475,top=28,row=16.2,max=15;
 [0,3,6,9,12,15].forEach(function(v){var x=x0+v/max*plotW;el("line",{x1:x,y1:24,x2:x,y2:274,stroke:"#e6e6e6","stroke-width":0.7});el("text",{x:x,y:15,"text-anchor":"middle","font-size":9,fill:"#777"},v+"%")});
 data.forEach(function(d,i){var y=top+i*row;if(i===11){el("line",{x1:10,y1:y-4,x2:660,y2:y-4,stroke:"#b6b6b6","stroke-dasharray":"3,3"})}el("text",{x:x0-8,y:y+10,"text-anchor":"end","font-size":9,fill:"#333"},d[0]);el("rect",{x:x0,y:y,width:d[1]/max*plotW,height:11,rx:1.5,fill:i===0?"#d4a52c":i<11?"#4a5568":"#8b9aa8"});el("text",{x:x0+d[1]/max*plotW+6,y:y+9,"font-size":9,"font-weight":700,fill:"#333"},d[1].toFixed(1)+"%")});
 el("text",{x:118,y:291,"font-size":9,fill:"#777"},"National and provincial rates above the rule; demographic comparisons below");
