})();
</script></div><div style="font-size:10px;color:#888;padding:5px 14px 12px;font-style:italic;">Source: Statistics Canada, Labour Force Survey, October 2026, released November 6. All 15 observations are reported rates; demographic categories overlap with provincial populations. | hdq.ca</div></div></div><p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;">Chart: Unemployment is the percentage of each group''s labour force without work and actively seeking employment. The first eleven bars are the national rate and ten provinces; the final four are separate demographic comparisons, not additional geographies. All values are seasonally adjusted.</p><h2>Why Friday''s Market Rally Does Not Cancel the Warning</h2>
<p>Reuters reported that the TSX rose 0.5% Friday, led by materials and technology, after Thursday''s steep decline. Materials gained 2.6% on the day and 11.4% over the week, while technology advanced 1.8% Friday. The same report placed US crude at $90.83 a barrel, down sharply over the week despite a modest Friday rise. Equity leadership therefore reflected sector-specific repricing, not a clean vote of confidence in Canadian consumer spending.</p>
<p>The policy backdrop is complicated by the November 5 federal budget. Ottawa announced plans for major capital investment and affordability measures, but proposed spending and tax incentives take time to affect employment and are not equivalent to enacted, immediately available household cash flow. TD Economics observed on Friday that the weak jobs report challenged the Bank of Canada''s stronger growth outlook, while the budget did not fundamentally alter its policy calculus. Neither interpretation guarantees the December decision.</p>
<h2>The Advisor''s Weekend Test Is Cash Flow, Not Market Direction</h2>
<p>Advisors should separate three exposures that clients often treat as one: portfolio volatility, job or business-income interruption, and debt repricing. A client whose salary depends on retail demand may need a different liquidity reserve from a retiree whose income comes from government benefits and a diversified bond ladder. A younger household with variable-rate debt and uncertain employment prospects has a different vulnerability from an established business owner who benefits from resource exports.</p>
<p>For the coming week, the practical discussion is whether emergency reserves cover a plausible period of interrupted income, whether near-term maturities require forced sales, and whether the client''s equity exposure is unintentionally concentrated in the same Canadian sectors that support their livelihood. Friday''s index recovery does not answer those questions. The most useful message of the week is that softer employment can support valuations through interest-rate expectations while weakening the real incomes that ultimately support spending and earnings.</p>',
